WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.960
Julia or Python? If you're trying to pick out a&nbsp;
programing language for your next project, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:14.040
not an easy choice, but let me help you decide. If&nbsp;
you're looking into a programing language to dive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:21.840
into data science, Python is a strong contender.&nbsp;
It's become the language for AI, machine learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.060
and all things numbers. And it's easy to see&nbsp;
why. It's got a gentle learning curve. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:33.660
available on just about any computing platform.&nbsp;
And thanks to a very active user community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:44.280
Python has tons and tons of sample source code out&nbsp;
there. There's really not a whole lot you can't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.180
with Python. At first glance, Julia and Python&nbsp;
might seem very similar. They do have a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:01:00.600
similarities. Both are relatively high-level&nbsp;
languages. They're also both open source.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:11.100
And they also share a characteristic of being&nbsp;
dynamically typed languages-- that applies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.520
to both of them. And that means that they can&nbsp;
be used for data science applications. And look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.980
even the way that you write code for Julia looks&nbsp;
kind of similar to the way that you write code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:31.620
for Python. But there are a few key differences&nbsp;
to be aware of. First of all, Julia is all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:42.480
performance. Now, of course, no language starts&nbsp;
out with the goal of being slow. But Julia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:51.000
compiles down to efficient native code through&nbsp;
the use of LLVM, which is a series of compiler and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.340
toolchain technologies designed around optimizing&nbsp;
code compilations for specific platforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.200
Python code can be compiled into&nbsp;
byte code for some performance gains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.480
but the advantage still goes to Julia.&nbsp;
When benchmarked against other languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.120
in a series of tests performing common code&nbsp;
patterns like sorting, parsing integers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:19.620
matrix multiplication and statistics, the Julia&nbsp;
compiled code consistently ran faster than Python.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:26.340
One of Julia's main stated goals from the very&nbsp;
beginning was to be competing with compiled C,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.080
which has long been seen as the benchmark&nbsp;
for highly performant optimized code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:41.400
So that's Julia's -- performance. One of&nbsp;
Python's greatest strengths is its extensibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:49.200
What do I mean by extensibility? Well, after&nbsp;
we've worked out the core element of our projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.380
we can bring in any number of additional libraries&nbsp;
to extend the core functionality of the language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:05.640
So maybe we've worked out some pretty clever AI&nbsp;
logic. We've got some AI logic here. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:13.140
simply by including a few new Python libraries, we&nbsp;
can extend that coding to include some-- let's see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.240
--those are glasses. OCR, so they can read&nbsp;
text on its own. And then perhaps we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:29.040
introduce some robotics to help it get around.&nbsp;
All that functionality is just a library call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:39.720
away and it means that Python really is the "do&nbsp;
anything" language. It really is very extensible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.840
However, that extensibility is perhaps why it lags&nbsp;
behind Julia in the performance category. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.240
Python provides a basic functionality and&nbsp;
gains its superpowers through the use of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:57.480
libraries. So for that benchmark that I mentioned&nbsp;
earlier, Python is using things like numPy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:07.140
for all of its matrix statistics and&nbsp;
multiplications. And then it's using OpenBlas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:14.880
for some other work, like the basic-- that's the&nbsp;
B --linear algebra sub-programs. So these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.380
all libraries that it's needing to use. And these&nbsp;
are extremely powerful and widely used libraries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.180
but using them does require knowledge of&nbsp;
those specific libraries, whereas Julia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:30.180
attempts to bring many of those capabilities and&nbsp;
functionalities into the Julia language itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.920
And that gives Julia a certain advantage&nbsp;
on the speed and simplicity side of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.940
With regards to the code that you type and&nbsp;
read, neither is terribly complex or confusing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.920
but they both follow a familiar and easy-to-read&nbsp;
format. They're both dynamically typed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.820
though Julia does something interesting,&nbsp;
which is called "multiple dispatch"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.520
with its typing. Now that's where different&nbsp;
methods can be used to handle different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:09.300
combinations of data types. So, for example, if&nbsp;
we have the addition of two floats-- so float&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:16.980
plus float --that can be handled differently&nbsp;
and optimized differently than the addition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:29.100
something else, say, two integers or even a string&nbsp;
plus an integer. Now that means with multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:36.180
dispatch, the method most specifically applicable&nbsp;
to the data type in the argument-- say five plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:42.900
three --is matched to the optimization method&nbsp;
that is most optimal, in this case int plus int.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:49.740
At the end of the day, the decision is up to&nbsp;
you. Python is just about available everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.840
that there's a process of capable of processing&nbsp;
ones and zeros. It's like your your local general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.540
contractor who's been around for several decades.&nbsp;
They've seen every problem and if they can't solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.560
it themselves, they at least have a subcontractor&nbsp;
they can bring in to get the job done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:10.260
Julia started off as a general purpose language,&nbsp;
but it's really finding its success in the field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:16.080
of data science and machine learning. It's like&nbsp;
the younger upstart who built their approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.540
around modern tools and techniques to get more&nbsp;
done thanks to specialization and efficiency. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:27.360
it's also been around long enough where it can't&nbsp;
be simply ignored as a flavor of the week. As far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.160
as I can see, Python will be the language to&nbsp;
learn, if you're going to learn a language. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.720
provides a strong set of functionality&nbsp;
that only gets stronger when extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.480
through libraries and frameworks, where Julia is&nbsp;
built with performance as its top priority. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:49.380
it really comes down to where you need to go and&nbsp;
how important it is that you get there quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.020
If you have any questions, please drop us&nbsp;
a line below. And if you want to see more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.580
videos like this in the future, please&nbsp;
like and subscribe. Thanks for watching.

